        "content": "Media was an ancient region associated especially with western and northwestern Iran. Its boundaries changed, and the surviving evidence does not support a single precise outline for every period. Hamadan, known in Greek sources as Ecbatana, became its best-known political center. Modern maps that give the Medes a sharply bounded empire can therefore suggest more certainty than the historical geography permits. [s1]\n\nAssyrian records reveal a landscape of numerous local principalities rather than an immediately recognizable centralized state. Assyria was the powerful kingdom based in northern Mesopotamia whose armies repeatedly campaigned into the Iranian highlands. Understanding those encounters helps explain why the Medes first become visible in external written sources: the records describe people whom Assyrian rulers fought, threatened, or sought to subordinate. They are evidence of contact and power, rather than an impartial survey of Median society. [s1][s3]",
