        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the written record establishes",
        "content": "Assyrian treaties from 672 BCE still identify local Median princes. Later Greek tradition attributed the foundation of Median kingship to Deioces, a judge who became ruler, but that narrative should not be treated as a contemporary administrative record. The contrast between local rulers in Assyrian documentation and the later story of unified kingship is central to the problem of reconstructing Median state formation. [s3]\n\nMedian forces nevertheless played an important part in the destruction of Assyrian power in the late seventh century BCE. This establishes their significance in regional warfare without, by itself, resolving how their political organization worked. [s3]\n\nA more direct account survives for the defeat of Astyages, the Median ruler, by Cyrus. The Babylonian text known as the Nabonidus Chronicle reports that Astyages’s army rebelled, delivered him to Cyrus, and that Cyrus subsequently took valuables from Ecbatana to Anshan. The relevant entry is conventionally assigned to 550/549 BCE. Because the tablet is damaged, reconstructed wording must remain distinguishable from fully preserved text. [s4]",
