        "content": "Godin Tepe and Nush-i Jan are important sites for investigating the period around Hamadan. Excavations have revealed substantial mud-brick architecture, including columned halls, storage areas, and fortified structures. Such buildings help reconstruct local authority, gathering spaces, and the organization of settlements. [s2]\n\nCalling a site “Median,” however, is an interpretation based on its location, date, and historical context. It does not mean that excavators have recovered a label identifying every inhabitant. Pottery also varies across the region; there is no simple, uniform ceramic signature that securely identifies all Medes. Architectural comparisons can suggest connections with earlier highland buildings and later Persian palaces, but resemblance alone cannot establish a complete institutional genealogy. [s2][s3]\n\nThe most useful way to approach the Medes is therefore through several kinds of evidence together. Written records establish particular encounters and rulers; archaeology reveals settlements and buildings. Their combination produces a substantial history, while leaving important questions about political integration unresolved. [s1][s2][s3]",
