        "id": "orientation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A history across changing political maps",
        "content": "Medieval Iran is best approached as a connected historical region whose political centers shifted over time. This guide follows the regional dynasties of the ninth century through the Timurid courts of the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries. It includes places beyond present-day Iran because cities such as Bukhara, Samarqand, and Herat were central to the political and cultural worlds discussed here. [s1][s4]\n\nDynastic names provide useful signposts, but they do not describe an uninterrupted succession of governments ruling identical territory. The Metropolitan Museum’s regional chronology, for example, places Ghaznavid power in Khorasan alongside Buyid rule farther west, and later distinguishes Khwarazmshah authority in the northeast from other regional formations. A single date range for a dynasty cannot substitute for a map of where its authority operated. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-dynasties",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Provincial authority and Persian literary patronage",
        "content": "The Tahirids governed Khorasan under the Abbasid caliphs, the rulers of the Islamic polity centered in Iraq. Their hereditary provincial position illustrates how substantial local authority could develop while loyalty to the caliphate continued. The Saffarids, based in Sistan, followed a more confrontational military course and took Khorasan from them. These were different political relationships, rather than successive expressions of a single national program. [s1]\n\nThe Samanids made Bukhara a major center of Arabic and Persian culture. Their patronage helped Persian become a prominent language of poetry, historical writing, and scholarship. Their political world also depended on military recruitment and the enslavement of captives; some commanders of enslaved origin acquired considerable power. The emergence of the Ghaznavids belongs partly to this changing military environment. Cultural achievement and coercive institutions therefore need to be considered together. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seljuk-world",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Seljuk conquest and the life of artistic traditions",
        "content": "The Seljuks, a Turkic dynasty with Central Asian nomadic origins, defeated the Ghaznavids at Dandanakan in 1040 and took Baghdad in 1055. Their expanding power connected Iran with Iraq and Anatolia. Subsequent divisions among branches of the ruling family demonstrate why an empire’s initial expansion should not be confused with permanent political unity. [s2]\n\nArchitecture and objects reveal connections that outlasted that unity. An iwan is a vaulted hall open on one side; courtyards organized around four iwans became especially important in mosque architecture. The congregational mosque of Isfahan preserves influential Seljuk interventions. Kashan’s ceramics and Khorasan’s inlaid metalwork likewise illustrate regional centers of technical accomplishment. [s2]\n\nThe label “Seljuk art” requires care. Museums also use it for works made after the principal sultanate’s political strength had declined. An artistic classification can describe a continuing repertoire of materials, forms, and techniques without proving that a particular ruler commissioned an object. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mongol-transition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Conquest, destruction, and Ilkhanid rebuilding",
        "content": "The Khwarazmshahs were an important northeastern power before Mongol conquest transformed the region. The invasions and the subsequent establishment of Ilkhanid rule were distinct stages: military devastation preceded a new dynasty’s consolidation in western Asia. The fall of Baghdad in 1258 ended the Abbasid caliphate there. [s3][s5]\n\nThe Ilkhanids were a branch of the Mongol ruling house whose power centered in northwestern Iran. Ghazan’s conversion to Islam in 1295 was an important development in court patronage. Illustrated books, ceramics, metalwork, and monumental buildings became prominent vehicles for combining established practices with East Asian motifs. The mausoleum of Oljeitu at Soltaniyeh is a major surviving architectural example. These achievements should be understood alongside the destructive conquests that preceded them. [s3]\n\nThe united dynasty broke apart after 1335, although some art-historical periodizations extend the Ilkhanid label later. Political collapse and the end of an artistic tradition did not occur on the same schedule. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "timurid-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Timurid courts and the geography of culture",
        "content": "Timur established his power in Transoxiana, the region beyond the Oxus River in Central Asia, in 1370. His conquests extended across greater Iran and neighboring territories. After his death in 1405, maintaining this extensive empire proved difficult; rival branches eventually ruled its remaining Central Asian and Khorasanian territories. [s4]\n\nSamarqand and Herat were major centers of Timurid patronage. Rulers and princes supported artists, architects, calligraphers, and manuscript workshops. Herat’s importance is a reminder that the history of Persian culture cannot be confined to today’s Iranian borders. Court libraries brought together the copying, compilation, and illustration of books, while religious foundations supported substantial building programs. [s4]\n\nThese surviving monuments and manuscripts offer a practical route into the period. Read their locations, patrons, and dates together: they reveal relationships that a list of rulers alone cannot explain. They also preserve the activities of wealthy patrons more readily than the experiences of the wider population, a limitation that should remain visible when interpreting medieval achievement. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
